\section*{1. Project Objective}
Our project evaluates whether Pagoda, a provenance-graph host intrusion detection system, detects original attack graphs differently from mimicry-evasion graphs. We partially reproduce the Pagoda experiment reported by Goyal et al.~\cite{goyal2023}, using the researchers' released implementation and graph datasets~\cite{repository}. Rather than rebuilding Pagoda or generating new attacks, we execute the released detector on selected benign, attack, and pre-generated evasion graphs. We also extend our evaluation to previously unseen benign graphs to examine false alarms.

\section*{2. Methodology and Progress}
We configured the project on Windows 11 using Python 3.11.9, a virtual environment, PyTorch on CPU (no CUDA), and 16 GB RAM. We extracted the released CSV datasets and wrote scripts to prepare reproducible experiment folders. The available dataset includes 71 benign training graphs, a separate benign test split, and released attack and evasion graphs. We left Pagoda's detection logic unchanged and kept saved artifacts separate across runs.

Our experiments proceeded in five stages: (1) a one-graph pilot to verify execution; (2) a five-benign-graph training configuration with five attack and five evasion graphs; (3) a 71-benign-graph training configuration using the \emph{same} attack and evasion samples; and (4--5) held-out benign evaluations using the saved artifacts from each configuration. For the held-out tests, we selected five unseen benign graphs (ADM-370, ADM-371, ADM-372, ADM-373, and ADM-375) and applied our evaluation script to Pagoda's saved frequency database and thresholds.

We inspected the released pipeline, including frequency-database construction, path extraction, scoring, threshold selection, and statistics reporting. A key implementation detail is that the printed values \texttt{fpr}, \texttt{tpr}, and \texttt{evasion} are \emph{counts}, not percentages. We also resolved dependency and data-extraction issues, handled intermediate artifact locations, and checked runtime on our CPU-only environment. Cached reruns took approximately 27 seconds for the 71-graph detector run and 31 seconds for the held-out evaluation; these are not fresh-training benchmarks.

\section*{3. Results}
Table~\ref{tab:results} summarizes our main results. The one-graph pilot completed successfully, detecting both its original attack and evasion graph; its primary purpose was to validate the pipeline.

