With five benign training graphs, Pagoda detected all five original attacks and all five evasion graphs. With 71 benign training graphs, it still detected all five original attacks but detected none of the five evasion graphs. Thus, the evasion success rate in our selected sample changed from 0/5 to 5/5. This is a \emph{qualitative partial reproduction} of the mimicry-evasion behavior studied by Goyal et al.~\cite{goyal2023}, not a reproduction of their full-scale quantitative evaluation.

The graph threshold increased from approximately 0.1038 to 0.1237 between configurations, while the path threshold remained near $1.0\times10^{-4}$. In our additional held-out test, all five unseen benign graphs were flagged under both configurations, by the graph-level check rather than the path-level check. Their graph scores under the 71-graph configuration were approximately 0.443--0.470, exceeding its 0.1237 threshold.

\section*{4. Discussion and Limitations}
Our main observation is that changing the amount of benign training data coincided with a reversal in the five evasion verdicts, even though the attack and evasion samples were held constant. The higher graph threshold is a plausible contributor, but the learned frequency database also changes. Without logging and comparing each evasion graph's score under both configurations, we cannot attribute the reversal to the threshold alone.

The held-out evaluation revealed a second concern: all five selected unseen benign graphs were flagged. This is a 100\% false-positive rate \emph{within our five-graph custom test only}; it is not an estimate of Pagoda's general false-positive rate. The discrepancy between zero training-benign graphs flagged and five held-out graphs flagged shows why in-sample counts should not be used as evidence of generalization. Our evaluation procedure may differ from the original experimental protocol and warrants further validation.

Several limitations remain. We evaluated only five original attacks, five released evasion graphs, and five held-out benign graphs. We did not generate the mimicry graphs ourselves, verify one-to-one correspondence between selected attack and evasion files, or measure the number of added events per matched pair. Furthermore, inspection of the released threshold-calibration code indicated that attack scores contribute to calibration, so the attack detection measurements are not fully independent of threshold selection. Finally, cached rerun times do not represent a clean end-to-end reproduction from scratch.

Another limitation was our available computing hardware. Our experiments were conducted on a Windows 11 computer with 16 GB of RAM, using Python 3.11.9 and a CPU-only installation of PyTorch because GPU acceleration was unavailable. These resource constraints influenced our decision to evaluate five attack graphs and five mimicry-evasion graphs rather than the full set of 100 graphs in each category. Although we successfully processed all 71 benign training graphs, we did not benchmark the complete 100-graph evaluation. Consequently, our experiments demonstrate the feasibility of a smaller-scale reproduction but do not establish the computational performance or detection outcomes of the full evaluation.

Overall, we successfully configured and executed the released Pagoda pipeline, reproduced mimicry-evasion behavior on a small selected sample, and added an independent benign-data check that exposed an important evaluation limitation. Next steps would be to log per-graph scores, validate attack/evasion pairing, and extend the held-out evaluation to the remaining benign test graphs.

